---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, REPO, RELEASES, VERSION } from '../lib/meta';

const title = 'Mulchie: keep your machine usable while AI agents build';
const description =
  'Open source supervisor for machines running many AI coding agents: monitor, build queue, budgets, hooks and safe cleanup. macOS app, CLI, local API, MCP.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Mulchie',
  description:
    'A macOS menu bar app, CLI, local HTTP API and MCP server that keeps one machine healthy while many AI coding agents run on it.',
  url: `${SITE}/`,
  applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'macOS, Linux',
  softwareVersion: VERSION,
  license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  downloadUrl: RELEASES,
  codeRepository: REPO,
  sameAs: [REPO],
};

---
<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd} ogImage="home">
  <div class="wrap hero">
    <div>
      <h1>Keep your machine usable while AI agents build on it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Mulchie shows what uses CPU, memory and disk per repo, worktree and agent, queues heavy builds, holds agents to budgets you set, and cleans up what they leave behind, with a plan you approve first.</p>
      <p class="who">For developers who run Claude Code, Codex, Cursor agents and similar tools side by side on one Mac or Linux box.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/install/">Install Mulchie</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={REPO}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    {/* TODO: replace this rendering with a real dashboard screenshot once one exists.
         Use an <img> with explicit width and height to avoid layout shift. */}
    {/* The sample output below has the format of the real command; the figures are made up and the figure says so. */}
    <figure class="term-fig" style="margin:0">
      <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Example output of mulchie status">
        <div class="term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>illustrative output</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie status</span>
health <span class="a">strained</span>  ·  disk 142 GB free of 460 GB  ·  memory 6% free  ·  cpu 94% (10 cores)  ·  swap 3.1 GB
4 agent process(es), 5 build process(es) across 4 workspace(s)

  api/wt-auth                                        310% cpu    2.1 GB  1 agent · 2 build
  api/wt-billing                                     280% cpu    1.8 GB  1 agent · 2 build
  web/wt-search                                      190% cpu    1.4 GB  1 agent · 1 build
  api/wt-old-spike                                     0% cpu    0 KB  0 agent · 0 build

<span class="p">$</span> <span class="caret"></span></code></pre>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <section class="sec wrap split" aria-labelledby="problem">
    <div>
      <h2 id="problem">Ten agents, one machine</h2>
      <p>Each agent gets its own git worktree and starts its own build. The CPU pins, memory goes to swap, and the disk fills with <code>target/</code> directories nobody is using.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>Every worktree has its own caches.</strong>Rust <code>target/</code>, <code>.next</code> and <code>node_modules</code> are often gigabytes each, and they stay after the agent finishes.</li>
      <li><strong>Agents do not know about each other.</strong>They start heavy builds at the same time because nothing tells them the machine is busy.</li>
      <li><strong>Activity Monitor shows processes, not projects.</strong>You see <code>rustc</code> at 300%, not which repo or which agent started it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="sec wrap" aria-labelledby="does">
    <h2 id="does">What it does</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <div>
          <h3>Monitor</h3>
          <p>Samples every 3 seconds and keeps 30 minutes of history. Every process is attributed to its repo or worktree from its working directory, then classified as an agent, a build or a runtime. <code>mulchie top</code> groups processes by app, workspace and program, and <code>mulchie diagnose</code> explains what is going on and what to do about it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Example output of mulchie top --processes">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie top --processes -n 3</span>
<span class="d">    PID   CPU%       MEM      IO/s   NI KIND     NAME             GROUP</span>
  48211  <span class="a">212.0</span>    1.4 GB      0 KB   10 build    rustc            api/wt-auth
  48907  <span class="a">188.0</span>    1.1 GB    12 KB   10 build    rustc            api/wt-billing
  39120   41.0    0.9 GB      0 KB    0 agent    node             web/wt-search</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div>
          <h3>Optimize</h3>
          <p>Lowers the priority of build processes when CPU or memory is under pressure. Runs a machine-wide build queue: <code>mulchie run</code> waits for a free slot and sets a fair <code>CARGO_BUILD_JOBS</code>. <code>mulchie advice</code> says whether now is a good time for heavy work, with reasons.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Example output of mulchie run and mulchie advice">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie advice</span>
<span class="a">wait</span>: all 4 build slots are taken
<span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie run -- cargo build</span>
<span class="d">mulchie: waiting for a build slot: 1 job(s) ahead in the queue</span>
<span class="d">mulchie: slot granted after 41s</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div>
          <h3>Reclaim</h3>
          <p>Finds stale build caches and idle git worktrees, and shows size, idle days, uncommitted files and a verdict for each. "Idle" means no file changes outside <code>target/</code>, <code>node_modules/</code> and <code>.git/</code> and no recent commit, not the folder timestamp. <code>mulchie prune</code> runs a package manager's own prune command for npm, bun, pnpm, yarn, uv, pip, Homebrew, Go and simulators.</p>
        </div>
        {/* Format matches the real `mulchie worktrees` output; the figures are made up. */}
        <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Example output of mulchie worktrees">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie worktrees</span>
   6.4 GB    0d   0 dirty  api › wt-auth      <span class="g">agent or process running here</span>
   4.1 GB   21d   0 dirty  api › wt-old-spike <span class="a">clean and merged; safe to remove</span>
   2.2 GB   14d   4 dirty  web › wt-checkout  <span class="a">idle 14d with 4 uncommitted file(s); removal saves a patch first</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec wrap split" aria-labelledby="supervise">
    <div>
      <h2 id="supervise">Supervise the agents, not just the machine</h2>
      <p>Mulchie groups each agent with everything it started into a session, so you see what Claude Code or Codex is using, not just <code>rustc</code>. It is a supervisor. It is not a sandbox and not an antivirus, and an agent that wants to get around it can.</p>
      <p class="note"><a href="/docs/sessions/">Sessions and limits</a>, <a href="/docs/hooks/">hooks and guard rules</a> and <a href="/docs/updates/">update checks</a></p>
    </div>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>Limits report by default.</strong>A budget caps CPU, memory, processes, writes or runtime per session. The action is <code>notify</code> unless you pick <code>deprioritize</code>, <code>pause</code> or <code>stop</code>. The agent itself is spared unless you say otherwise, and only the engine holding the lock acts.</li>
      <li><strong>Hooks for five agents.</strong>Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI and Copilot CLI can tell Mulchie when a session starts and before each shell command. A hook fails open: if Mulchie is down or slow, the agent carries on.</li>
      <li><strong>Guard rules are a tripwire.</strong>They warn, or block if you ask, on risky shell commands such as <code>curl | sh</code> or a force-push. They read command text, so they miss obfuscated commands.</li>
      <li><strong>Update checks never install.</strong>The engine asks GitHub and npm for newer versions and reports them. Turn it off with <code>updates.check off</code>. Vulnerability lookups are off unless you enable them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="sec wrap split flip" aria-labelledby="safety">
    <div>
      <h2 id="safety">It deletes things, so it checks first</h2>
      <p>Cleaning up after agents only works if you can trust it with your repos. You see a plan first, and every item is checked again at the moment it runs, not decided from an earlier scan.</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>Backups before removal.</strong>Uncommitted changes are saved as a patch. Ignored files such as <code>.env</code> are archived. A detached <code>HEAD</code> gets a <code>backup/</code> branch. Branches are never deleted.</li>
        <li><strong>Projects in use are left alone.</strong>If a process runs anywhere in a project, nothing in it is cleaned or removed.</li>
        <li><strong>Narrow deletes.</strong>Only recognised build caches under your scan roots, by canonical paths, never through symlinks.</li>
        <li><strong>Careful signals.</strong>Only your own processes, after a plan you confirm. Never pid 1, never Mulchie itself, never operating-system programs.</li>
        <li><strong>Localhost only.</strong>The API listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, needs a bearer token with mode <code>0600</code>, and refuses requests that carry a browser <code>Origin</code> header. A read-only token can read and plan but not change anything.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><a href="/docs/safety/">Read the full safety model</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Example output of mulchie sweep --dry-run">
        <div class="term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>example output</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie sweep --dry-run</span>
Plan: remove 2 worktree(s), 6.3 GB
  remove     4.1 GB  api › wt-old-spike  (idle 21d; removable)
  remove     2.2 GB  web › wt-checkout  (idle 14d; removable)
                    <span class="a">saves patch of 4 changed file(s)</span>
Not ready (1):
  skip   api › wt-auth  <span class="g">node (pid 39120) is working in /Users/me/dev/api/wt-auth</span> [busy]</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec wrap split" aria-labelledby="agents">
    <div>
      <h2 id="agents">Agents use it too</h2>
      <p>Agents talk to the same engine you do. One command adds the MCP server to Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor or Codex. Agents start with a diagnosis, ask whether to start a build or whether a command is risky, take a build slot, and plan cleanups that you approve. Deleting is always two calls: a plan, then execute. Add <code>--read-only</code> for an agent that should look but not act.</p>
      <p>The dashboard has a ready-made snippet for your <code>AGENTS.md</code>. There is also a local HTTP API for scripts and <code>curl</code>.</p>
      <p class="note"><a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP tools</a> and <a href="/docs/http-api/">HTTP API</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack" style="display:grid;gap:1.5rem">
      <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Connect an agent over MCP and hooks">
        <div class="term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>connect an agent</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie mcp install --client claude-code</span>
<span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie mcp install --client cursor --read-only</span>
<span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie hooks install --client claude-code</span></code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="term" role="group" aria-label="Queue a build">
        <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie run -- cargo build</span>
<span class="p">$</span> <span class="k">mulchie run --label web -- bun run build</span></code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec wrap" aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
    <p class="muted">The app needs macOS 12 or later. The CLI, engine and API run on macOS and Linux.</p>
    <ul class="cmds">
      <li>
        <div class="label">Menu bar app<span>Homebrew cask</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>brew install --cask williamrogersdev/tap/mulchie</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="label">CLI only<span>Homebrew</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>brew install williamrogersdev/tap/mulchie-cli</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="label">CLI on macOS or Linux<span>Install script</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>curl -fsSL https://mulchie.com/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="label">CLI from source<span>Cargo</span></div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>cargo install mulchie</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cta" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/install/">All install options</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={RELEASES}>Download the DMG</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={REPO}>GitHub</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
